confirmed.fix:
Drop the disjunct and stop photographing the entrance for this claim - assert the declared timeline instead of a sampled frame, which removes the flake as well as the vacuity. Inside the existing ?motion=play block in interaction-check.mjs, replace line 534 with:

  const seq = await p2.evaluate(() => {
    const g = (el, name) => {
      const a = el.getAnimations().find((a) => a.animationName === name);
      if (!a) return null;
      const ct = a.effect.getComputedTiming();
      return { start: Number(ct.delay), end: Number(ct.delay) + Number(ct.activeDuration) };
    };
    const cols = Array.from(document.querySelectorAll('.plot .colw'));
    return {
      firstCol: g(cols[0].querySelector('.bar'), 'grow'),
      lastCol: g(cols[cols.length - 1].querySelector('.bar'), 'grow'),
      record: g(document.querySelector('.record'), 'draw'),
    };
  });
  ok('the old ceiling does not start until every column has started and the first has finished',
    Boolean(seq.record && seq.record.start > seq.lastCol.start && seq.record.start > seq.firstCol.end), JSON.stringify(seq));
  ok('the old ceiling finishes after the last column has finished',
    Boolean(seq.record && seq.record.end > seq.lastCol.end), JSON.stringify(seq));
  ok('and it is fully drawn once the entrance is over', Number(late.record) === 1, String(late.record));

Measured on the master: firstCol {340,840}, lastCol {780,1280}, record {1100,1700} - both ordering assertions true. Driven against `.record{animation-delay:0s}` they are both false (record {0,600}); driven against `.record{animation:none}` getAnimations() returns nothing and the Boolean() guard makes both false. That is a guard that fails for the thing it names, which line 534 never did. The third line keeps a live-paint check with no fallback disjunct, so the pair covers both 'declared in the right order' and 'actually painted'.

